2020 is obviously front and center and  two of the Democratic presidential  candidates appear to poll appear by  polling to have a chance at beating  president Trump that would be senator  Bernie Sanders and former Vice President  Joe Biden so while both candidates have  a slight edge over the  commander-in-chief most surveys show  Biden as the front-runner  senator Sanders comes in as the second  choice here via Skype to discuss this  trend is David Pakman he's a political  commentator and host of the David Pakman  show David thank you so much for joining  us great to be with you David one of the  things we're taking a look at on the  show a lot is about how Bernie Sanders  comes in is one of the I think he's the  number two choice for several of the  candidates within the field basically  making it poised so if that there is a  bite and implosion or a dropout or  what-have-you he would likely be the  nominee what is what are you taking a  look at in terms of this polling what do  you think it reveals about the race yeah  a couple different things one is that if  you look historically so August of 2015  which is sort of like where we are right  now in 2020 if you look at oddest of  2011 August of 2007 typically whoever is  leading primary polling right now goes  on and ends up being the nominee but  there have been some pretty close calls  so for example with the Democrats back  in 2016 meaning in late 2015  Bernie Sanders actually came all the way  back to get within the margin of error  of tying Hillary Clinton and then  started dropping and as we know Hillary  Clinton ultimately became the nominee so  one thing to pay attention to is that  even though the the sort of most likely  scenario is that Biden the leader right  now in most polling ends up being the  nominee it doesn't necessarily have to  be that way historically it could be  could be warned as well as Bernie  Sanders and interestingly often when you  ask people who do you think will  ultimately be the nominee as opposed to  saying who do you support  polling is more accurate in that way and  one way to look at that is the  prediction markets where people are able  to actually kind of bet bet with their  money as to who they think will be the  nominee and Joe Biden is not actually  leading there it's actually Elizabeth  Warren recently who has taken the lead  there and  that's an interesting turn of events I  think we should pay attention to I think  that is fascinating I mean lay on the  pack for her because one of the  challenges she has to overcome is she  still has a mostly sort of white  educated liberal coalition and obviously  you can't win the primary without  expanding to have a large base among  people of color too so what do you see  is the path for her  yeah I've not seen that particular data  not that it doesn't exist I just haven't  seen it because I think at this point  it's it's less important than who is  being substantive and pushing policy in  depth and that's basically how she's  picked up about a point to a point and a  half a month going all the way back to  March which is she put out a  extraordinarily detailed proposal about  education she put out a detailed  proposal about lowering wealth and  income inequality she recently put one  out about the internet public option and  restoring net neutrality and she's been  actually gaining without any you know  debate killshot attempts without sort of  campaign ads or superficial stuff she's  just been going out and talking to  voters including in red states that  Donald Trump won in 2016  and talking about policy which is a very  different approach than the other  candidates have seen who have sort of  jumped and fallen much more erratically  it's been a slow and steady gain for  Elizabeth Warren on policy David one of  the things you're also known for is very  good media analysis I have noticed that  Elizabeth Warren has generally received  pretty favorable coverage in the  mainstream media but that Bernie Sanders  certainly has had his own you know his  own quibbles with them we have a latest  story from the New York Times about why  Bernie Sanders stood out at the Iowa  State Fair it claims that he didn't want  to make personal contact with voters  despite drawing some of the biggest  crowds there I mean just yesterday  Bernie went after the Washington Post  for coverings covering some of the labor  disputes within his campaign claiming  that they were doing so because they  were owned by Amazon's CEO Jeff Bezos  the world's richest man what do you make  of this media coverage and then the  Senators response and how he's handling  that yeah I mean I think that the thing  I would sort of zoom out a little bit  which is that when we look at the  interactions of candidates with voters  in  general both Bernie Sanders and  Elizabeth Warren do really well in front  of crowds where they are talking about  policies so if you look at Bernie  Sanders and his Fox News Town Hall that  he did where he had an entire room of  people selected for that Town Hall  raising their hands saying yeah I would  prefer the plan that you're proposing  instead of what we have now the Fox News  host didn't know what it hit him it  looked like they were in a twilight zone  is that what's going on they're like  this is we can't ever do this again he  actually gets to talk to people  Elizabeth Warren is going in to talk to  crowds as I said in red states so you  know I don't know exactly what happened  at this fair I've not been to many of  these fairs and don't really know what  the dynamics are like but big picture he  is a candidate that's connecting with  voters just like Elizabeth Warren is  yeah yeah I mean I think that's what  people well you know raising a skeptical  eyebrow at least Sanders supporters  about this piece to say the least  because here he goes the Iowa State Fair  by a lot of accounts he draws the  largest crowd he is way more grassroots  donors in the state than any other  candidate and this is the angle that  they choose to take seemed a little  strange but I also wanted to get your  thoughts on there's a conversation  happening right now around Joe Biden and  his gaffes or slips of the tongue or  whatever you want to call it seem to be  coming at a very frequent pace and are  raising questions in people's minds  about whether he in fact is the  candidate to be Donald Trump if he is  the most quote-unquote electable one do  you think that this sort of thing could  ultimately hurt him or do you think it's  kind of baked into the Joe Biden cake  well listen I mean the the recent  comment Joe Biden made about I think it  was poor kids or just as smart as white  kids and then he sort of fumbled and  said oh I mean wealthy kids and Latino  kids and Asian kids or whatever else to  say that that's evidence that Joe Biden  holds some kind of deep-seated racist  belief I think goes too far I guess  certainly it's evidence that it's sort  of a slower and more tired Joe Biden  than we've seen it's a Joe Biden who as  we know may not sort of like be  sensitive to the 2019 way of talking  about some of these things but as far as  electability the most recent poll on  that is a survey USA poll which has both  Biden and Bernie  52:42 over Trump in a hypothetical  national election now that's only 92% of  the vote obviously those are not going  to be the final numbers there's 8% there  that at some point is going to coalesce  behind either the Democratic nominee  Trump or some third party I don't think  that the Democratic nominee is going to  win the popular vote by eight but  looking back in 2016 where these numbers  are starting to resemble what they could  be which is that you could have a  Democratic nominee be a Biden or Bernie  or Warren or whoever else who does win  the popular vote by two or three points  as Hillary Clinton did but could win or  lose the electoral college and that's  why I've been saying this is ultimately  goin